    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d34">

                <pb facs="0107" n="67" sameAs="frus:frus1865p1/pg67"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Adams</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1201]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-12-26"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December</hi> 26<hi rendition="#italic">,</hi>
                        1864</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatch of December 1, No. 823, has
                    been received, together with a copy of a note which Earl Russell has written to
                    James M. Mason, John Slidell and Dudley Mann. If the present government of her
                    Majesty were less just or generous towards the United States than they are, I
                    might think it wise to raise a question upon that extraordinary
                    communication.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD,</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Francis Adams,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
